WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.628 --> 00:00:03.222
- [Narrator] Forty miles
southwest of Tucson

00:00:03.222 --> 00:00:05.796
between Arizona's Coyote
and Baboquivari mountains

00:00:05.796 --> 00:00:09.138
is an old west ranch town like no other.

00:00:09.138 --> 00:00:12.667
Cowtown Keeylocko was built
by this man, Ed Keeylocko.

00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:14.742
- [Ed Keeylocko] That's
Edward J.B. Keeylocko.

00:00:14.742 --> 00:00:16.595
- [Narrator] All with his bare hands.

00:00:16.595 --> 00:00:19.095
- [Ed Keeylocko] That's right.

00:00:22.412 --> 00:00:24.236
- [Narrator] Like any old
west town, Cowtown Keeylocko

00:00:24.236 --> 00:00:28.069
has a church, a bank, a
cemetery and a saloon.

00:00:30.633 --> 00:00:33.554
But unlike most other old
west towns, this Cowtown

00:00:33.554 --> 00:00:36.932
is still standing, all
because of this man.

00:00:36.932 --> 00:00:40.576
- I drink to that.

00:00:40.576 --> 00:00:42.818
I've built everything
here, everything you see

00:00:42.818 --> 00:00:46.220
round here was done with a small truck.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:49.021
I had no machinery, but I
figured out how to do it.

00:00:49.021 --> 00:00:52.077
Even that water tower, that one there,

00:00:52.077 --> 00:00:54.008
hauled that up with a pickup truck.

00:00:54.008 --> 00:00:54.884
- [Narrator] Why exactly did Edward

00:00:54.884 --> 00:00:57.434
J.B. Keeylocko build his own town?

00:00:57.434 --> 00:00:59.704
Ed, born to a black
mother and a white father,

00:00:59.704 --> 00:01:02.280
was orphaned at a young
age in Jim Crow America.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.655
- My childhood was rough.
I was light-skinned,

00:01:04.655 --> 00:01:08.504
red hair, green eyes.

00:01:08.504 --> 00:01:11.507
I can remember a young
kid climbed a tree once, he told me,

00:01:11.507 --> 00:01:15.692
“Ed, look up here, I’m up here,”
and he urinated in my face.

00:01:15.692 --> 00:01:18.823
And those are the kind of
things that you'd have to

00:01:18.823 --> 00:01:21.658
live with, cause you, if you look like me.

00:01:21.658 --> 00:01:23.248
- [Narrator] From these
hardships, Keeylocko developed

00:01:23.248 --> 00:01:26.847
his independent frontier
spirit, wouldn’t you say so, Ed?

00:01:26.847 --> 00:01:28.508
- I'd paddle on my own
canoe, like they say in the West,

00:01:28.508 --> 00:01:32.675
I sat on my own broncs,
I rode it out, I’ve done all of this.

00:01:33.646 --> 00:01:34.926
- [Narrator] With this lesson in mind,

00:01:34.926 --> 00:01:37.418
Ed constructed an old west utopia.

00:01:37.418 --> 00:01:39.309
Four decades later, Cowtown continues

00:01:39.309 --> 00:01:40.979
its celebration of individuality

00:01:40.979 --> 00:01:43.887
with the biannual event
known as Keeylocko Days.

00:01:43.887 --> 00:01:46.562
- [Ed Keeylocko] People like
to come to Cowtown Keeylocko.

00:01:46.562 --> 00:01:50.094
There's no hatred here, no racism;

00:01:50.094 --> 00:01:52.261
we accept you who you are.

00:01:53.502 --> 00:01:56.355
You can dream the things that 
never were and say why not?

00:01:56.355 --> 00:01:59.967
That's the way I, in my mind, thinks.

00:01:59.967 --> 00:02:02.061
Lot of people thought I was crazy.

00:02:02.061 --> 00:02:05.811
I suppose maybe I am.
Maybe I am, maybe I am.

